WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
This lesson walks through the Claude Code tool system, meaning the core, built-in tools you'll reach for the most.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.582
What each one does, when Claude picks one tool over another, and why Bash is the most powerful and

3
00:00:13.582 --> 00:00:15.932
the most dangerous tool in the set.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The tool system is how Claude Code actually does things, so these tools are foundational.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.225
Every action Claude Code takes flows through a tool call, and each call is one discrete action

6
00:00:28.225 --> 00:00:31.933
Claude selects, based on what the current step requires.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
The tool system is the full set of built-in capabilities it can invoke. Read, write, and edit handle files.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Bash executes shell commands. Glob and grep find files and text. Web search and web fetch reach the web.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.681
Agent delegates a subtask to a subagent, with its own separate context window. Two of these earn a

10
00:00:54.681 --> 00:00:56.931
closer look later in this lesson.

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.163
Edit makes targeted find and replace changes, while write replaces a whole file, and agent is what

12
00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:06.937
turns Claude Code from a single worker into an orchestrator.

13
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
We'll come back to both.

14
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Read is the safest tool in the set. It views the contents of a file without making any modifications.

15
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.266
One clarification worth locking in. Read operates on files, not directories. If Claude needs a

16
00:01:22.266 --> 00:01:25.933
directory listing, it uses bash with a command like ls.

17
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.566
If it needs to find files by name or by content, it uses glob or grep. Read itself returns a file's

18
00:01:33.566 --> 00:01:37.923
contents, including rendered images, PDFs, and notebooks.

19
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Claude typically runs read before edit to confirm the current state of a file before changing it.

20
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.557
That read before edit pattern prevents a common class of errors, where Claude acts on stale

21
00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:51.938
assumptions about what a file contains.

22
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
It has no modification risk by design, so reading the relevant files before modifying them is standard practice.

23
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Write and edit are both file modification tools, but they behave very differently.

24
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Write overwrites the entire file. Every byte of existing content is replaced with the new content.

25
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
If you use write on an existing file and leave out 10 lines, those 10 lines are gone. There's no partial update.

26
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.600
Edit works differently. It takes a target string and a replacement string and performs a scoped find

27
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.944
and replace on the file.

28
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Only the matched section changes. Everything else stays intact.

29
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.556
For most code changes, edit is the right tool. Use write for creating new files, or for intentional

30
00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:40.933
full rewrites where you want to replace everything.

31
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Conflating them is an exam trap worth knowing.

32
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Bash is the most capable tool and the most dangerous one. It executes any shell command.

33
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Running scripts, installing packages, moving or deleting files, starting processes, querying databases, calling APIs.

34
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
There's no built-in constraint on what it can affect.

35
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.097
A poorly scoped bash command can delete files, corrupt state, or make irreversible changes to the

36
00:03:09.097 --> 00:03:10.926
system with no undo path.

37
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:18.000
The permission model and hooks are the primary controls. Without them, bash has wide reach across the system.

38
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.542
Understanding this is essential for designing Claude code workflows that run in automated or

39
00:03:24.542 --> 00:03:28.939
production environments where a bad bash call could cause serious damage.

40
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Web Search lets Claude query the web for information that goes beyond what it knows from training.

41
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Current documentation, recent API changes, error messages from new library versions, release notes.

42
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
These are all things that Web Search can retrieve that Claude's training data might not cover.

43
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Results come back as text that Claude reads and reasons about before acting.

44
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.803
Claude selects Web Search when it determines that the task requires information it can't reliably

45
00:03:58.803 --> 00:03:59.940
know from training.

46
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
It's not always invoked, only when the current task has a knowledge gap that a web query can fill.

47
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:13.000
Web Search extends Claude code from a local codebase tool to one that can pull in current external information.

48
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.000
The agent tool spawns a sub-agent with its own separate context window.

49
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:26.000
The parent session delegates a sub-task to the sub-agent, which runs independently and returns a result.

50
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.114
The main use cases are tasks too large to fit in the current session's context window, and tasks

51
00:04:32.114 --> 00:04:35.936
that are isolated enough to run in parallel with other work.

52
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Agent is the tool that turns Claude code into a multi-agent orchestrator, the parent coordinates.

53
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
The sub-agents do the isolated work.

54
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.837
Because each sub-agent has its own context window, you can handle much larger tasks by decomposing

55
00:04:52.837 --> 00:04:57.930
them into parallel sub-tasks and delegating each one to a separate agent.

56
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Tool selection in Claude code is task-driven.

57
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Claude doesn't pick tools based on capability alone.

58
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.000
It picks the tool that matches what the current step requires.

59
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Inspecting existing state, before acting, read.

60
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Changing specific lines in a known file, edit.

61
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Creating a new file or completely rewriting one, write.

62
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Running a command, script, or system operation, bash.

63
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Looking up current information that training data won't have, web search.

64
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Delegating an isolated sub-task to its own context, agent.

65
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.000
The planning loop runs this selection decision at every step.

66
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.798
Getting tool selection right is what makes a multi-step workflow execute coherently, rather than

67
00:05:42.798 --> 00:05:45.939
reaching for the wrong capability at the wrong time.

68
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Every tool in the set does one narrow job, and Claude repicks between them at each step of the task.

69
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:02.000
That's why a request phrased slightly differently can take a completely different route through your files.

70
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:07.000
One thing to watch. Bash is the exception to that narrowness.

71
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.000
It'll run whatever you hand it, so scope it before you loosen anything else.

72
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
That does it for this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

